#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf4ad931-d6c5-5a3d-914c-c276a009d84c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156611} {:answer/id #uuid "cbaf6f33-be77-5b1a-8c72-98ed6a1a8fb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156612} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc0cd67-7df9-568a-bc9b-0c7d65892443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156613} {:answer/id #uuid "e35981b8-f69e-5e88-946e-767277ca3b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156614} {:answer/id #uuid "17d4dea2-d5ec-5f4c-8bfc-1c46ee872135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17d4dea2-d5ec-5f4c-8bfc-1c46ee872135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156615} :answer-field/id #uuid "799ba412-3bb6-5597-813b-29c7be335d75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78e1f8f9-d432-5676-a7be-df1e563ec326" :question/math-academy-id "q-88209" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{6}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-9}^{9}2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =3(-x)^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2} \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=5{x}^{3}$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2{x}^{5}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =2(-x)^{5} \\\\  & =-2{x}^{5} \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-9}^{9}2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}]}
